<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chupan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1327">1327</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Amir Chupan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chupan was a powerful Mongol military commander, or amir, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> in the early fourteenth century. He held effective power during the minority of Abu Sa'id, who came to the throne in 1317. His family founded the Chupanid dynasty, one of the successor states that emerged after the Ilkhanate fragmented.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Chupan came from the Suldus clan and descended from Chilaun, son of Sorqan Shira, one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>'s four great companions. His grandfather Tudaun went west with Hulagu, governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyar-bakr">Diyar Bakr</a> and died at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbistan">Elbistan</a> in 1277, and his father Malek took part in the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1258. In his twenties Chupan fought against Nogai of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> in 1289. He supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaykhatu">Gaykhatu</a>'s bid for the throne and in 1295 changed sides to join Ghazan against Baydu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under Ghazan he served as a senior commander in three campaigns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, then ruled by the Mamelukes, and after the defeat at Marj al-Saffar in 1303 he stayed with the army when its commander Qutlugh Shah fled. In 1307 he led one of four armies against the rebellious province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> and persuaded the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astara">Astara</a> and Gaskareh to surrender. When Qutlugh Shah was killed there, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oljeitu">Öljeitü</a> made Chupan Amir of the Ulus, his chief military commander, and he married Öljeitü's daughter Dowlandi Khatun on 30 September 1307.</p><p>Under Abu Sa'id he turned on the vizier Rashid-al-Din Hamadani, who was put to death in July 1318. In 1319 he drove back an invasion by Öz-Beg of the Blue Horde and defeated a conspiracy led by Abu Sa'id's uncle Irinjin near Mianeh. His sons Timurtash, Shaikh Mahmud, Hasan and Demasq Kaja then received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, and Chupan married Abu Sa'id's sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sati-beg">Sati Beg</a>. In November 1325 he made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a>, repairing the water system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and founding a bath and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The extravagance of Demasq Kaja, the hostility of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> such as Chupan's protégé Rukn al-Din Sa'in, and Chupan's refusal to let Abu Sa'id marry his daughter Baghdad Khatun, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-buzurg">Hasan Buzurg</a>, turned the ruler against him. On 25 August 1327 Abu Sa'id had Demasq Kaja killed. Chupan marched west for revenge, but near Ray most of his army deserted, and he withdrew east. The Kartid ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, Ghiyath ud-Din, received him as a friend and then had him strangled on Abu Sa'id's orders, sending one of his fingers as proof. Baghdad Khatun had him buried in the Baqi cemetery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. Sources describe him as a devout Muslim who rebuilt mosques in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> and demolished churches, yet protected Mar Yahballaha III.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chupan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successor Abu Saʿid Bahadur Khan came to the throne as a minor in 1317, which left effective power with military emirs such as <b>Amir Chupan</b>, whom he eventually overthrew in 1327.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Amir Chupan</b> was the most powerful commander of the Ilkhanate under Öljeitü and Abu Sa'id until Abu Sa'id had him killed in 1327.</blockquote><a href="/state/chupanids">Chupanids</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.34,62.2&amp;year=1317" aria-label="Herat on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6132 920 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Chupan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6582 1190h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6582 1190h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=34.34,62.2&amp;year=1317">What was here in 1317? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
